WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.595
Food security and sovereignty.

00:00:02.545 --> 00:00:04.397
The ninth dimension.

00:00:04.397 --> 00:00:08.693
Three companies control over sixty percent of Canada's grocery market.

00:00:08.693 --> 00:00:11.784
They fixed bread prices for fourteen years.

00:00:11.784 --> 00:00:14.863
Food inflation has outpaced wage growth.

00:00:14.863 --> 00:00:18.636
One in four Canadian children experience food insecurity.

00:00:18.636 --> 00:00:23.238
Northern and Indigenous communities pay two to three times southern prices.

00:00:23.238 --> 00:00:29.238
Canada exports agricultural products globally but cannot ensure its own population eats affordably.

00:00:29.238 --> 00:00:38.045
This is the ninth dimension of sovereignty failure: a nation that cannot feed its people affordably is not food sovereign, regardless of its agricultural output.

00:00:38.045 --> 00:00:50.170
The grocery oligopoly, combined with supply chain concentration and the housing crisis that consumes family budgets, creates a food security crisis that harms the most vulnerable — especially children and Indigenous communities.

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:53.159
Three dimensions of food sovereignty failure.
